# Expectations and Error Analysis

## Initial work ideas

This project first aimed to produce an accurate IPA translation based only on acoustic characteristics of audio_files.
To do that, the best approach is using Machine Learning because speech is temporal so coarticulation must be considered. 
And today, the best way to deal with it is ML models (e.g. wav2vec 2.0)
Nevertheless, I realized I didn't have enough time and material to build an IA from scratch so I decided to use Allosaurus, the reference of Speech-to-IPA nowadays, and to focus on one of its weak points : tones.

## Tones in IPA transcriptions

So here we are, why tones are a weak point of these models ?
Because, to make a model Speaker-agnostic, the creators often decide to ignore F0, the pitch information, which enables us to recognize a woman, a child or a man speaking. The thing is : F0 also contains tone informations.

Then, to retrieve this information, we should either search it in the layers of a ML model where it still hasn't been suppressed, or measure it again and extract it. 
I chose to measure it again with the help of Parselmouth, a python library that links the code with the software Praat : I check timestamps provided by Allosaurus, to see where exactly in the audio I should search for F0.
After that, I use the Z-score to measure the f0 mean of the speaker in the audio, the standard-deviation and compare the mean to every F0 values in the given timestamps.

I map those F0 values with various tones and add them to the tone-naked IPA transcription from Allosaurus.

## Evaluation

To compare the output of Allosaurus only and the one adding tones, I use the Phone Error Rate (PER) and an expert IPA transcription associated to the audio file.
The lower the better because it adds the number of substitutions, deletions and insertions divided by the total number of phones in the expert transcription.

Here are some results : 
The audio, from the UCLA Phonetics Archive, lasts 18 seconds.
Its language is Zulu (isocode : zul), a bantu language with 28 millions speakers from South Africa. 
The expert IPA transcription provided with the audio is the following : lìmá lìmá lùndùzɛ́là lùndùzɛ́là lálà lálà àsíséfì sɛ̀fá sɛ̀fá
The one done by Allosaurus is this one (I added spaces approximately): lim lim lunuzɛ lunluzɛ lala lala jsisifhi sɛf sɛfɔ
And the one with tones : lím lím lúnùzɛ́ lúnlùzɛ́ làlà làlá jsísífhì sɛ̀f sɛ́fɔ́
Evaluation:
PER (Allosaurus)            : 54.72%
PER (Allosaurus with tones) : 37.74%

## Limitations of this work : 

1. If Allosaurus provides an incorrect IPA transcription, the tone distribution won't be accurate.
2. To prevent Allosaurus from making incorrect IPA transcriptions, we should use in priority languages from which Allosaurus has the allophonic inventory.
3. When evaluating the performance, the allophonic inventories in PHOIBLE (used by Allosaurus) should be the same as the one used in the expert IPA transcription attached with the audio, which is not always the case.
4. I wished to create a language-agnostic tone recognition system but it is not possible with this script yet because in order to provide a tonal transcription similar to the expert one, we have to precise the number of possible tones and which one precisely are in the language to ensure that the output will only provide these. Because, in practice, the algorithm will detect other tones and try to put them on IPA symbols while their are not "existing" in the language.
5. The script only works on noise-free audios because I didn't have time to implement a noise reduction during the F0 extraction and test it accurately.
6. For the model to guess the approximate mean of F0 in the voice of a speaker (and then compare it to extremes that he/she produced), the audio should ideally contain every possible tones in the language, and only one speaker voice. 
